---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import ContactSection from '../../components/ContactSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';

// Wording is carried over from the current live page. Changes from the live page:
//  - Employer threshold now reads "three or more people" and the notice/filing deadlines are
//    stated correctly (30 days to tell the employer, two years to file, exceptions apply),
//    matching the owner's legal review of the Workers' Comp page. The live page said to notify the
//    employer "within two years" and "more than two employees".
//  - Death benefits use the reviewed Workers' Comp page wording (no dollar figure).
//  - "court" wording reworded: the Commission holds hearings and is not a court.
//  - Live FAQ promised a hearing "within thirty days"; replaced with a plain statement. Needs Bryn's review.
//  - Live FAQ about a "second injury fund" replaced with a general statement about aggravated
//    old injuries. Needs Bryn's review.
//  - Photo from the old page left out (source unknown).
const title = 'Virginia Factory Accident Attorney | Factory Accident Claims';
const description =
  'After a factory accident, you need a Virginia workers’ compensation lawyer you can trust. Call Pathfinder Injury Law in Richmond today.';
const h1 = 'Virginia Factory Accident Lawyer';
const path = '/virginia-factory-accident-lawyer/';

const toc = [
  { id: 'injuries', label: 'Common Factory Accident Injuries in Virginia' },
  { id: 'eligible', label: 'Am I Eligible for Workers’ Comp Benefits After a Factory Accident?' },
  { id: 'benefits', label: 'Your Benefits for a Virginia Factory Accident' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'Virginia Factory Accident FAQ' },
  { id: 'help', label: 'Hurt in a Factory Accident?' },
];
---
<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  crumbs={[
    { name: 'Workers’ Compensation Lawyer Serving Virginia', path: '/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/' },
    { name: h1, path },
  ]}
  overlay
  author
>
  <PageHero
    title={h1}
    crumbs={[{ name: 'Workers’ Comp', href: '/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/' }, { name: 'Factory Accidents' }]}
  />

  <div class="wrap page-body">
    <article class="prose">
      <p class="lead">
        If you’re hurt in a factory accident in Virginia, you have a chance to get the benefits you
        need to recover through Virginia workers’ comp.
      </p>
      <p>
        When you’re hurt at your job in a Virginia factory, the results can change your life.
        Amputations, paralysis, and other severe injuries can have permanent effects, making it tough
        to recover from your injuries. Here’s how a
        <a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/">Workers’ Comp claim</a> works after a factory accident.
      </p>
      <h2 id="injuries">Common Factory Accident Injuries in Virginia</h2>
      <p>
        Your Virginia factory accident can lead to some severe injuries that may take a little more than
        rest to cure. You may suffer from permanent, painful injuries that impact your quality of life.
        In some cases, you may lose an important sense like sight, or you may be unable to take care of
        yourself after the accident.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your factory accident lawyer in Virginia can step in when you’re injured and help you with
        your claim for medical care and lost wages. Below are some common injuries you may have suffered in a
        Virginia factory accident:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/virginia-back-injury-lawyer/">Back injuries</a></li>
        <li>Brain injuries</li>
        <li>Spinal cord injuries</li>
        <li>Broken bones</li>
        <li>Traumatic amputations</li>
        <li>Eye damage</li>
        <li>Musculoskeletal injuries</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="eligible">Am I Eligible for Workers’ Comp Benefits After a Factory Accident?</h2>
      <p>
        When you’re seriously hurt in a factory accident, you may not know where to start. Are you even
        eligible for Workers’ Comp benefits? How do you get them? Getting these answers now can help you
        prepare for your hearing with the Virginia Workers’ Compensation Commission.
      </p>
      <p>
        The short version is, most workers in the state are eligible for Workers’ Comp benefits. If your employer has three or more employees regularly in service, they are generally required to carry that coverage, although some exceptions apply. If not, your factory accident lawyer can determine
        whether your employer is breaking the law.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once you know you’re eligible for compensation, getting started is easy. You must
        <a href="https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title65.2/chapter6/section65.2-600/" rel="noopener">report your injury to your employer within 30 days</a>
        and
        <a href="https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title65.2/chapter6/section65.2-601/" rel="noopener">file a claim with the Commission within two years</a>.
        Missing either deadline can cost you your benefits, although exceptions can apply.
      </p>

      <div class="pull">
        <p><strong>Learn how we can help you with your factory accident Workers’ Comp case.</strong></p>
        <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="#contact">Get Help Now</a></p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="benefits">Your Benefits for a Virginia Factory Accident</h2>
      <p>
        Worried about getting benefits after a Virginia factory accident? If you’re unsure what
        benefits you’re due, it’s easy to accept an agreement that doesn’t meet your needs following
        your factory accident. Because of this, you need to know what coverage you’re eligible for and
        talk it over with your factory accident lawyer.
      </p>
      <p>
        First, you shouldn’t have to pay out of pocket for your doctor visits when you’re hurt on the
        job. Benefits may include reasonable and necessary medical care and part of your lost wages. That’s
        not just your initial treatment, either. Travel costs, rehabilitation, and specialist
        visits can be included too, when they are reasonable, necessary, and related to the accident.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you’re unable to work, you still need money for your needs. Your bills won’t stop just
        because you can’t work. Because of this, you may need disability benefits, which replace a
        portion of your weekly wages. The wage replacement benefits you get will depend on the details
        of your claim, like whether you’re able to return to work at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        What if you’re not the one injured in a factory accident? Maybe you recently lost a loved one to
        a fatal factory accident. That puts you in a difficult position financially because you may have
        relied on them, as your spouse or guardian, for your financial needs. Death benefits can pay for a limited amount of
        funeral expenses, medical bills incurred before the death, and lost wages for certain periods of
        time for certain survivors or dependents who meet statutory criteria.
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq">Virginia Factory Accident FAQ</h2>
      <p>
        After a factory accident, you may have more questions than answers. All you’re sure of is that
        you’re hurt, and you need benefits to recover from your injuries. Here are the questions we hear
        most.
      </p>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>When can I expect my benefits from my accident?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              When you’re hurt in a Virginia factory accident, you need Workers’ Comp benefits now, not
              later. If you have not heard back about your claim, ask the
              insurance company for its decision in writing. If your claim is denied, you have the right to request a hearing before the
              Virginia Workers’ Compensation Commission so you don’t have to keep waiting for your
              Workers’ Comp coverage.
            </p>
            <p><a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/how-long-do-virginia-workers-comp-settlements-take/">Learn More</a></p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What if my employer denied my Virginia Workers’ Comp claim?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              Sadly, you may have received a letter about your Workers’ Comp benefits, only to find
              you’ve been denied compensation. But that doesn’t mean you’re out of options. You can ask for a
              hearing before the Commission, where a deputy commissioner hears the evidence and decides
              whether benefits are due. Here’s how to
              <a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/how-to-prepare-for-a-virginia-workers-comp-hearing/">prepare for a hearing</a>
              and get your <a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/">Workers’ Comp benefits</a>.
            </p>
            <p><a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/what-happens-if-your-workers-comp-claim-is-denied/">Learn More</a></p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>In Virginia, can I get all my Workers’ Comp benefits right now?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              In some cases, you may want a
              <a href="/should-i-accept-a-lump-sum-settlement-for-my-workers-comp-claim/">lump-sum settlement</a>
              for your Virginia factory accident, rather than weekly payments. A lump sum means you’ll
              receive a compromised payment of your benefits all at once. Settling your case closes
              everything and ends benefits, so you may be unable to get further benefits for your injury if you’re not healing as fast as your doctors thought.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What if I don’t have a new injury from the accident, but my old injury is worse?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              In some cases, your factory accident may not have caused a new injury. But that old back
              injury flared up, or a previous head injury made your concussion much worse. In these
              situations, your injury may be more severe than it would have been. Workers’ comp can
              cover a pre-existing condition that a work accident made worse, but the medical records
              have to show what the accident changed.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do I need a factory accident lawyer?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              When you’re hurt in a factory accident, you may not have the answers you need. Sure, you
              know to tell your employer about the accident, but what about the insurance company? What
              if your claim is going to a hearing? A lawyer is not required to file a claim. Factory
              claims often turn on the machine, the guard, and the safety rules: a willful breach of a
              known, reasonable safety rule can bar recovery, and the employer has the burden of proof.
              Attorney fees in Workers’ Comp cases are subject to the approval and award of the
              Commission.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does Virginia Workers’ Comp pay for lost wages?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              After a serious work injury in Virginia, you may be directed to your Workers’ Comp
              benefits to recover from your injuries. You can’t cover your bills and needs without your
              wages, so it’s vital that you get coverage.
            </p>
            <p>
              Yes, in part. If the insurance company accepts your claim for lost wages, it sends you an
              Award Agreement. Once that is signed and the Commission enters an award, you are paid for
              the time you are unable to work because of the injury, starting on day 8, and if you are
              out of work more than three weeks, the first seven days are paid too
              (<a href="https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title65.2/chapter5/section65.2-509/" rel="noopener">Va. Code § 65.2-509</a>).
            </p>
            <p><a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/does-virginia-workers-comp-pay-for-lost-wages/">Learn More</a></p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What happens when you file a Workers’ Comp claim?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              After a serious work accident, you may have immediately reported your accident to your
              employer. They need to know about injuries that happen at work, and they’re responsible
              for your Workers’ Comp benefits.
            </p>
            <p>
              After you file, the Commission will order your employer’s insurance company to respond to
              your claim within 30 days. It may accept the claim or deny it.
            </p>
            <p><a href="/what-happens-when-you-file-a-workers-comp-claim/">Learn More</a></p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What’s the Workers’ Comp Process in Virginia?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              Virtually all employers with three or more employees regularly in service in Virginia are required to carry workers’ compensation insurance. If an employee receives an injury while completing their job-related duties, they can file a claim with the Virginia Workers’ Compensation Commission to seek benefits including reimbursement of costs. Employees can use the Workers’ Comp process to apply for several
              financial benefits.
            </p>
            <p><a href="/workers-comp-process-in-virginia/">Learn More</a></p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <div class="pull" id="help">
        <h2>Hurt in a Factory Accident?</h2>
        <p>
          <a href="/">Pathfinder Injury Law</a> handles only Virginia workers’ compensation claims. Call
          <a href={site.phoneHref}>804-505-0633</a> or fill out our
          <a href="#contact">online contact form</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <aside class="side" aria-label="Page navigation and contact">
      <div class="side-card side-call">
        <h2>Free Consultation</h2>
        <a class="cta-phone" href={site.phoneHref}>{site.phoneDisplay}</a>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#contact">Get Help Now</a>
      </div>
      <nav class="side-card" aria-label="On this page">
        <h2>On This Page</h2>
        <ul class="toc">
          {toc.map((t) => <li><a href={`#${t.id}`}>{t.label}</a></li>)}
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </aside>
  </div>

  <ContactSection />
</Base>
